@* A callout that explains the thing next to it in words a child would understand, and then
   names the grown-up term for it underneath.

   Why the two halves matter: the plain sentence is what makes the screen usable by someone who
   has never seen it, and the technical line is what makes this an interview answer rather than a
   party trick. "We page in the database" is a claim; "we only ever carry ten sheets out of the
   filing cabinet, because carrying the whole cabinet is what makes screens slow" is an
   explanation. Both, in that order, is the format that lands.

   The component renders NOTHING when explain mode is off, so a page can be written with these
   sprinkled everywhere and still look like a serious admin tool to a colleague who never touches
   the switch. *@

@if (Mode?.Enabled == true)
{
    <aside class="explain">
        <span class="explain-emoji" aria-hidden="true">@Emoji</span>

        <div class="explain-body">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title))
            {
                <p class="explain-title">@Title</p>
            }

            @ChildContent

            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Grownup))
            {
                <p class="explain-grownup"><b>Grown-up words</b>@Grownup</p>
            }
        </div>
    </aside>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Supplied by the layout. Null when no provider is present, which reads as "off".
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Nullable on purpose. A component that throws because nobody cascaded a value is a component
    /// that cannot be dropped into a test host or a new page without ceremony.
    /// </remarks>
    [CascadingParameter]
    public ExplainMode? Mode { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Decoration for the callout. Carries no meaning, hence aria-hidden.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Emoji { get; set; } = "🧸";

    /// <summary>The question this callout answers, phrased as the reader would ask it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The explanation itself.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The same idea in the vocabulary an interviewer would use. Optional.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Grownup { get; set; }
}
